A||N||2||johnmac||V6 2.8L Timing Belt/Gear Inspection||||1||
Z||000000||johnmac||09-12-2000||04:23 PM||||How do I get at the timing Belt/Gear for an inspection. I am in the process of changing out my water pump and figured I do the entire process at the same time.<P>What should I look for?<P>Any suggestions?<P>John MacHaffie<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||09-12-2000||04:40 PM||||there's just a few more bolts. I think some of them are torx heads. they may be hidden behind the crank pully, which I think you'll have to pull anyways. If the chain seems excessively loose, or if the gears are the 'quiet' version (plastic coating) sometimes that plastic breaks down and lets them loosen up. I'd just say hey, as long as you got it torn down that far, just replace it to save ya another trip into the engine. Also, I can't remeber how the gasket is down there (my damn engine has been in the shop too long) but use care when pulling the TC cover off.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||09-12-2000||05:17 PM||||Here is what you'll find if you have the v-6 (at least it is what I had on my 85 V-6).<P>The timing chain cover is underneath the water pump.  Several water pump mounting bolts ( 6 I believe) go through the timing chain cover into the engine block.  The cover has an additional four bolts or so.  I'm pretty sure they were all hex head bolts. <P>You will have to remove the harmonic balancer to get the timing chain cover off.<P>When pulling the water pump off cut out a piece of cardboard or styrofoam and trace the outline of the new water pump on it marking the bolt holes.  As you remove the bolts/studs from the water pump stab them into the cordboard/styrofoam template so you remember which goes where.  There are several sizes and lengths of bolts that each has a particular place to go.  You'll be glad you did when it comes time to re-install the water pump.<P>On the earlier v-6s the timing chain cover has a sloped gasket surface that mates to the matching surface on the oil pan.  It takes a bit of care to get these surfaces clean and to get them sealed when you put it all back together.  I did my 85 when I installed a long block.  With the engine on an engine stand and everything clean and new it was easy.  Trying the same with the engine still in the car would be quite different.<P>Also if I remember correctly one of the long water pump mounting bolts actually threads into a water jacket and must be sealed with RTV.  I believe the instructions that came with my water pump covered that.<P>Hope this helps.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
